Here's the copy, plus the code that picks which message to show. I'm assuming the frontend calls `stripe.handleNextAction` as described earlier.

**Message when the customer cancels or fails the challenge**

> **Card verification wasn't completed**
> Your bank needs to verify this payment, and the verification was canceled or didn't go through. You haven't been charged. You can try again or use a different card.
>
> [Try again] [Use a different card]

It doesn't blame the customer, it says plainly that no money was taken, and it offers two ways forward. The "different card" option matters because some customers can't complete the challenge (no access to their banking app, SMS not arriving).

**Choosing the message**

```js
const MESSAGES = {
  authCanceled: {
    title: "Card verification wasn't completed",
    body: "Your bank needs to verify this payment, and the verification was canceled or didn't go through. You haven't been charged. You can try again or use a different card.",
    actions: ["retry", "change_card"],
  },
  declined: {
    title: "Your bank declined this payment",
    body: "The verification went through, but your bank still declined the payment. You haven't been charged. Please try a different card or contact your bank.",
    actions: ["change_card"],
  },
  generic: {
    title: "We couldn't complete your payment",
    body: "Something went wrong on our end. You haven't been charged. Please try again in a moment.",
    actions: ["retry"],
  },
};

async function run3dsChallenge(stripe, clientSecret) {
  const { error, paymentIntent } = await stripe.handleNextAction({ clientSecret });

  if (error) {
    if (error.code === "payment_intent_authentication_failure") {
      return { ok: false, message: MESSAGES.authCanceled };
    }
    if (error.type === "card_error") {
      return { ok: false, message: MESSAGES.declined };
    }
    return { ok: false, message: MESSAGES.generic };
  }

  switch (paymentIntent.status) {
    case "succeeded":
    case "processing":
      return { ok: true };
    case "requires_action":
    case "requires_payment_method":
      // Modal closed or authentication failed without an error object
      return { ok: false, message: MESSAGES.authCanceled };
    default:
      return { ok: false, message: MESSAGES.generic };
  }
}
```

**Things to check**

- **Verify the abandon path in test mode.** I'm not certain whether closing the modal returns an error or resolves with the intent still in `requires_action`. The code handles both, but confirm which one you get. Use a 3DS test card such as `4000 0027 6000 3184` and try both the modal's fail button and dismissing it.
- **"Try again" depends on the intent's state.** If it's still `requires_action`, call `handleNextAction` again. If it's `requires_payment_method`, the customer has to re-submit card details and you confirm again. The `next-action` endpoint's `action` field tells you which.
- **Don't clear the form** when showing this message, so a retry doesn't mean retyping everything.
- **Log the outcome** (canceled, failed, declined) as an analytics event. After you ship, that shows how many of the 3% recover versus abandon, which is the number you'll want to judge this change by.

